.TH DMENU 1 dmenu\-VERSION
.SH NAME
dmenu \- dynamic menu
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dmenu
.RB [ \-bcfiOv ]
.RB [ \-ix ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR lines ]
.RB [ \-g
.IR columns ]
.RB [ \-eh
.IR lines ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR index ]
.RB [ \-sep
.IR char ]
.RB [ \-W
.IR width ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR monitor ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR prompt ]
.RB [ \-fn
.IR font ]
.RB [ \-nb
.IR color ]
.RB [ \-nf
.IR color ]
.RB [ \-sb
.IR color ]
.RB [ \-sf
.IR color ]
.RB [ \-ob
.IR color ]
.RB [ \-of
.IR color ]
.RB [ \-bw
.IR width ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR windowid ]
.P
.BR dmenu_run " ..."
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dmenu
is a dynamic menu for X, which reads a list of newline\-separated items from
stdin.  When the user selects an item and presses Return, their choice is printed
to stdout and dmenu terminates.  Entering text will narrow the items to those
matching the tokens in the input.
.P
.B dmenu_run
is a script used by
.IR dwm (1)
which lists programs in the user's $PATH and runs the result in their $SHELL.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-b
dmenu appears at the bottom of the screen. This overrides centering.
.TP
.B \-c
dmenu appears centered on the screen.
.TP
.B \-f
dmenu grabs the keyboard before reading stdin if not reading from a tty. This
is faster, but will lock up X until stdin reaches end\-of\-file.
.TP
.B \-i
dmenu matches menu items case insensitively.
.TP
.B \-O
dmenu lists the matching items in the order they came on stdin, instead of
exact matches first, then prefix matches, then the rest (input_order in
config.h).
.TP
.BI \-l " lines"
dmenu lists items vertically, with the given number of lines (also when there
are fewer items, with fixed_lines in config.h).
.TP
.BI \-g " columns"
dmenu lists items in a grid of the given number of columns and
.B \-l
lines, filled column by column. Left and right select the item in the
neighbouring column.
.TP
.BI \-eh " lines"
each item of the vertical list is the given number of lines high and shows
that many lines of its text (see
.BR \-sep ).
.TP
.BI \-sep " char"
items on stdin are separated by
.I char
instead of a newline, so an item can have several lines.
.IR \e0 ,
.I \en
and
.I \et
are a NUL byte, a newline and a tab.
.TP
.B \-ix
dmenu prints the index of the selected item (0 is the first item on stdin)
instead of its text, and \-1 for the input text.
.TP
.BI \-n " index"
selects the item with the given index at start.
.TP
.BI \-W " width"
the width of the window in pixels, when centered.
.TP
.BI \-m " monitor"
dmenu is displayed on the monitor number supplied. Monitor numbers are starting
from 0.
.TP
.BI \-p " prompt"
defines the prompt to be displayed to the left of the input field.
.TP
.BI \-fn " font"
defines the font or font set used.
.TP
.BI \-nb " color"
defines the normal background color.
.IR #RGB ,
.IR #RRGGBB ,
and X color names are supported.
.TP
.BI \-nf " color"
defines the normal foreground color.
.TP
.BI \-sb " color"
defines the selected background color.
.TP
.BI \-sf " color"
defines the selected foreground color.
.TP
.BI \-ob " color"
defines the outline background color (for multiple selection).
.TP
.BI \-of " color"
defines the outline foreground color (for multiple selection).
.TP
.B \-v
prints version information to stdout, then exits.
.TP
.BI \-bw " width"
sets the width of the window border in pixels. The border and the line
between the input and the list use the SchemeBorder color of config.h.
.TP
.BI \-w " windowid"
embed into windowid.
.SH USAGE
Items are selected using the arrow keys, page up, page down, home, and end,
or the mouse: a click selects an item, a double click confirms it, the wheel
selects the previous or next item and a click outside dmenu exits, returning
failure.
.TP
.B Tab
Select the next item, wrapping around to the first.
.TP
.B Shift\-Tab
Select the previous item, wrapping around to the last.
.TP
.B Return
Confirm selection.  Prints the selected item to stdout and exits, returning
success.
.TP
.B Ctrl-Return
Confirm selection.  Prints the selected item to stdout and continues.
.TP
.B Shift\-Return
Confirm input.  Prints the input text to stdout and exits, returning success.
.TP
.B Super\-1 ... Super\-9
Confirm the first to ninth item shown.  Prints it to stdout and exits,
returning success.
.TP
.B Super\-r
Exit without selecting an item, returning failure (supercancelkey in config.h).
.TP
.B Escape
Exit without selecting an item, returning failure.
.TP
.B Ctrl-Left
Move cursor to the start of the current word
.TP
.B Ctrl-Right
Move cursor to the end of the current word
.TP
.B C\-a
Home
.TP
.B C\-b
Left
.TP
.B C\-c
Escape
.TP
.B C\-d
Delete
.TP
.B C\-e
End
.TP
.B C\-f
Right
.TP
.B C\-g
Escape
.TP
.B C\-h
Backspace
.TP
.B C\-i
Tab
.TP
.B C\-j
Return
.TP
.B C\-J
Shift-Return
.TP
.B C\-k
Delete line right
.TP
.B C\-m
Return
.TP
.B C\-M
Shift-Return
.TP
.B C\-n
Down
.TP
.B C\-p
Up
.TP
.B C\-u
Delete line left
.TP
.B C\-w
Delete word left
.TP
.B C\-y
Paste from primary X selection
.TP
.B C\-Y
Paste from X clipboard
.TP
.B M\-b
Move cursor to the start of the current word
.TP
.B M\-f
Move cursor to the end of the current word
.TP
.B M\-g
Home
.TP
.B M\-G
End
.TP
.B M\-h
Up
.TP
.B M\-j
Page down
.TP
.B M\-k
Page up
.TP
.B M\-l
Down
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR dwm (1),
.IR stest (1)
